      "text_raw": "The folded cascode OTA is\nalso an excellent first stage\nfor a two-stage Miller\nCMOS OTA. As usual the\nsecond stage is just one\nsingle transistor with active\nload.\nAs a result, the GBW is\nset by g and C . Now\nm1 c\nthere are two nondominant\npoles, however. The lower-\nfrequency one is normally at\nthe output. The other, at\nnodes 1 and 2 are usually at\nthe highest frequency.\nBecause of the second\nstage, the output swing can be rail-to-rail. Indeed, even when the output voltage is very close to\nthe positive supply voltage, and the output transistor M12 enters the linear region, and loses its\ngain, there is still sufficient gain remaining in the first stage to suppress the distortion.",
      "text": "The folded cascode OTA is also an excellent first stage for a two-stage Miller CMOS OTA. As usual the second stage is just one single transistor with active load. As a result, the GBW is set by g and C . Now m1 c there are two nondominant poles, however. The lowerfrequency one is normally at the output. The other, at nodes 1 and 2 are usually at the highest frequency. Because of the second stage, the output swing can be rail-to-rail. Indeed, even when the output voltage is very close to the positive supply voltage, and the output transistor M12 enters the linear region, and loses its gain, there is still sufficient gain remaining in the first stage to suppress the distortion."
    }
